<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest (plan 4.3). Required since May 2024.

  Three claims, all of them true of this app as the Android app is true of it:

    * NSPrivacyTracking false and no tracking domains. There is no analytics
      SDK, no advertising SDK and no crash-reporting SDK in this app, and the
      licences test fails the build if one ever appears.
    * NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes empty. Everything the app sends goes to the
      household's own server, which Coders Farm never sees; problem reports go
      by the person's own email, from their own mail app. Apple's definition
      of "collected" is data leaving the device in a way that lets US or a
      partner of ours get at it, which is nothing here.
    * The required-reason APIs below, each with a reason code from Apple's
      published list.

  When any of that stops being true — a phone lookup to Wikipedia, an HTTPS
  report destination of ours, any service of ours — this file and the App
  Store privacy label change in the same batch, and the privacy policy the
  same day, as for Play.

  NOT YET VERIFIED. This file has never been through a build, and the reason
  codes are quoted from the plan's reading of Apple's list rather than from
  Apple's page itself. The two marked TODO(verify-at-mac) are the ones to
  confirm first; a wrong code is rejected at upload, not at build, so it is
  worth checking before the first TestFlight build rather than after.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  UserDefaults. Settings, the chosen theme, the device name, the phone
		  bar's layout: the app's own preferences and nothing else. CA92.1 is
		  the code for an app reading and writing its OWN defaults, which is
		  all this app does (it is in no app group and shares nothing).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  File timestamps. The Files screen shows when a file was changed, the
		  audiobook and video downloads resume from a partial file by its date
		  (with ETag and Last-Modified), and saved media is listed by date.
		  C617.1 is the code for timestamps displayed to the person or used
		  inside the app's own container.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Free disk space. The model download (2.5 GB for Gemma 4 E2B) checks
		  there is room before it starts, and so does saving an audiobook or a
		  video to the phone: a download that fills someone's phone and then
		  fails is worse than a download that never starts. 85F4.1 is the code
		  for checking space before writing a file at the person's request.

		  TODO(verify-at-mac): confirm 85F4.1 against Apple's required-reason
		  list, and confirm it covers "the person asked us to save this, is
		  there room" rather than only a developer-initiated write.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>85F4.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  System boot time. Used only to measure elapsed time that a clock
		  change cannot bend: a timer counting down, a playback position, the
		  back-off between socket reconnections. Nothing about it leaves the
		  phone. 35F9.1 is the code for measuring elapsed time inside the app.

		  TODO(verify-at-mac): confirm 35F9.1, and confirm we actually need
		  this category at all — if every elapsed-time measurement ends up
		  going through `ContinuousClock` / `DispatchTime` rather than
		  `kern.boottime`, this whole entry comes out.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
